WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.994 --> 00:00:04.473
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.270
The thing about the
clarinet is that it

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.520
can have worlds of expression.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.270
And it can do that in so many
different styles of music,

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.600
from classical music
to jazz, to rock,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.770
to all kinds of world music.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.990
And I felt that this
project was a way

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.970
to remind everybody the
possibilities of it.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.710
We're going to
hear in one evening

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.200
the clarinet played by
many different people

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.350
in many different ways.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.060
We're hearing it
in a jazz setting,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.650
we're hearing it in
an orchestral setting.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.270
And I think that gave a nice
opportunity for the audience

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.024
to experience the full
breadth of this instrument.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.760
I was glad to be invited.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.370
And there's some other good
clarinet players playing,

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.729
so I'm happy to be here.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.340
The clarinet is a phenomenally
expressive instrument,

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.950
even though its tone tends
to be on the darker side.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:47.120
Any instrument that has
survived for centuries

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.420
is there because people
have consistently

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.370
found it to be a good
conduit for the channeling

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.010
of human thoughts and
feelings and ideas

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.990
and emotions into sound.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.090
My journey with the clarinet--

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:38.400
this little fella has been
with me for over 30 years now.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.800
I started to play jazz when
I got into high school.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:15.125
And I thought, I'm going to
be able to play clarinet.

00:04:15.125 --> 00:04:18.060
But clarinet went out
of fashion, apparently.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.529
I did not know.

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:21.360
So the teacher was--

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.814
he kind of discouraged me
from playing the clarinet.

00:04:23.814 --> 00:04:25.980
He said why don't you bring
your brother's saxophone

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:26.820
or any saxophone?

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.470
Just don't bring the clarinet.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.320
When I was real little,
my dad was a jazz fan.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.690
He'd always have-- he
listened to these saxophone

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.030
recordings of David Sanborn.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.800
Third grade is where you
would-- the earliest age you

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.490
could join the band program.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.550
And so every year they
would show the beginning

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.540
third graders and
have a presentation,

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.420
where the fourth and fifth
graders they would go and play

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.400
their instruments on stage
and the third graders

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:36.066
in the audience.

00:05:36.066 --> 00:05:38.430
And then it was to try to
convince them to join the band

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.020
and to pick an instrument.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.720
And so I kind of wanted
to play saxophone

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.916
because a little bit
of my dad's influence.

00:05:44.916 --> 00:05:46.290
That was his
favorite instrument.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:49.194
But the clarinet
was a lot smaller

00:05:49.194 --> 00:05:51.360
and then I realized that
it could fit in my backpack

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.280
and I wouldn't have to lug
around the heavier saxophone.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:18.780
So back in the
origin of jazz, back

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.190
in Dixieland and New
Orleans type jazz,

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.120
the clarinet was a very
prominent instrument.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.720
When jazz changed a lot from
the swing era to the bebop era,

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.810
there were no famous
bebopist clarinetists.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.525
You had Charlie Parker
on the saxophone.

00:06:30.525 --> 00:06:31.900
And that's part
of the reason why

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:33.040
the saxophone has
become probably

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.430
the dominant instrument in jazz.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.430
Clarinetists are
mostly in big bands

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.440
playing this older swing stuff.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:42.270
And so they didn't quite make
the shift over to the new era

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:42.970
immediately.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:45.610
There have been bebopist
this clarinetists since then,

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.560
but there were no real figures
for people to look up to,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:49.060
I guess.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:09.960
One, two, three, one.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:10.960
[SOUNDING MUSICAL NOTES]

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:12.460
You need to articulate.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:13.720
[SOUNDING MUSICAL NOTES]

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:15.070
It's all going somewhere.

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:16.291
Go.

00:07:16.291 --> 00:07:17.290
[SOUNDING MUSICAL NOTES]

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:17.789
OK.

00:07:17.789 --> 00:07:20.560
Well, it was another
one of Fred's ideas.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.450
This whole thing came
to me in a dream.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:24.910
And I can't believe
it actually happened.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.580
It hasn't happened yet,
but we've gotten this far.

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:29.830
So this is very, very cool.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:32.500
Last year he mentioned something
about a clarinet summit

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:36.160
where you would get Evan Ziporyn
and my instructor Billy Novick

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.170
and potentially some
other people to come play.

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:39.010
Don Byron.

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:39.580
Right here.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:45.540
Eran Egozy.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.070
Billy Novak.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.590
Evan Ziporyn.

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:54.190
Fred's idea was to have a
piece at the end of the concert

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:56.260
that would involve
all five players

00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:00.850
and that would be in some way
tailored to their strengths

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:03.270
and their inclinations,
their musical inclinations.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:06.120
And asked me if I could
write such a piece.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:14.900
If we're celebrating
the clarinet,

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.390
then why don't we
really go all the way

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:21.140
and celebrate it by
inviting people who play it?

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:23.540
I saw this concert advertised.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:25.520
Then I saw, if you bring
your own instrument,

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:26.510
you can play along.

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:27.560
And three--

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.809
The fact that we're
inviting clarinetists

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:41.669
from around town to
come and actually

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:44.629
play with us, that's
kind of unprecedented.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.100
If you just invite the
community at large,

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:55.580
then you're opening yourself
up to all levels of performance

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:58.430
ability and intonation and time.

00:08:58.430 --> 00:08:59.960
94.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:02.660
When to have these
16th note figures.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:04.370
Those should not be articulate.

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:05.360
It's really like,

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:06.429
[SOUNDING MUSICAL NOTES]

00:09:06.429 --> 00:09:07.220
That kind of thing.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.803
I mean, you tongue it, but the
airstream should not be broken.

00:09:09.803 --> 00:09:11.720
Really, up until the
performance it kind of

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:14.160
had that edge of
your seat quality.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.770
Like, is this even
going to work?

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:17.150
Is it going to fall apart?

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:21.010
I've been learning new notes
throughout the rehearsals.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:44.120
It's complicated.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:45.590
It's complicated music.

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:47.630
A lot of shifting time
signatures and things.

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:49.430
And I'm not used to that.

00:10:12.334 --> 00:10:14.000
I really love the
fact that this concert

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.300
is going to be, not just
about celebrating the clarinet

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:18.963
but celebrating community.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.660
To be able to play with these
great clarinetists, that's

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:29.750
just, you know,
once in a lifetime.

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:41.990
The rhythm to this
piece changes so often

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.450
that it's really fun to play.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:37.070
The whole thing about the
clarinet is that it-- to me,

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:43.040
is a singularly kind of 18th,
19th century contraption.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.950
If you look at it it's
basically a recorder

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:48.410
with a bunch of doo
hickeys attached to it.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:51.410
What I'm trying to do is
use all these contraptions

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:54.320
and use the acoustic nature
of the instrument-- it's just

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:56.750
this wooden tube that
has a lot of overtones

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:58.942
and harmonics hidden
in it, and just kind of

00:11:58.942 --> 00:11:59.900
bring those things out.

00:12:39.184 --> 00:12:40.600
What's idiomatic
on the instrument

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:42.820
are things like Mozart, like--

00:12:46.235 --> 00:12:48.360
But for me I'm much more
interested in finding ways

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:49.660
to get in the cracks.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:51.306
And so instead of
playing like scales,

00:12:51.306 --> 00:12:52.680
I much prefer to
do things like--

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:02.980
that's based on an
African Ya Titi.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.050
Or do things that try
to make the instrument

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:06.580
sound like a Shakuhachi flute.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:39.100
It's a wonderful program
and a great opportunity

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:42.070
and a wonderful
gesture that Fred

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:45.380
is bestowing upon
us, the clarinetists,

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:49.257
to shine a spotlight on
this great instrument.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:16.510
The clarinet's such
a nerdy instrument.

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:17.093
Let's face it.

00:15:19.535 --> 00:15:21.910
The clarinet's more difficult
to play than the saxophone.

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:24.330
So it's harder to play like
fancier stuff and complex

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:25.040
changes.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:27.630
And also the clarinet isn't
as loud as the saxophone

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.996
or the trumpet or
other instruments.

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:37.710
So it's like an
adult instrument.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:40.430
As far as the tone quality and
things you can do, they're--

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:42.375
may be much more
nuanced than sax.

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:42.440
But it also has a
tremendous range.

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:44.840
It has a range of
over three octaves.

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:46.440
It sounds different
in every range,

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:48.856
so you can have a variety of
sounds that can come from it.

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:54.335
I did not realize how
versatile of an instrument

00:16:54.335 --> 00:16:55.960
the clarinet was when
I first chose it.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.870
You have what's known as
the chalumeau register,

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:01.920
the low register.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:19.060
Very warm.

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:19.626
Very rich.

00:17:19.626 --> 00:17:21.750
Then you have like really
high, bright stuff like--

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.890
This is called the clarion
register, and clarion kind

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:28.640
of like a bell.

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:36.310
And then the altissimo register,
which is the next register.

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:44.490
A very kind of more powerful,
kind of penetrating, piercing,

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:46.560
annoying, people
might say sometimes.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:49.051
I like using different
registers to sort of capture

00:17:49.051 --> 00:17:50.925
different voices, almost
accompanying myself.

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:43.270
I started playing the
instrument because I had asthma

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:47.324
and my doctor said,
either I could swim

00:18:47.324 --> 00:18:48.740
or I could play a
wind instrument.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:50.870
And everybody knows
black people can't swim.

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:52.687
So I ended up playing
a clarinet then.

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:17.260
I got a good sound on it.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:21.610
It wasn't intended to be
a career move of any kind,

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:25.168
but then it kind of
ended up being that.

00:21:17.296 --> 00:21:18.670
When I play the
clarinet I really

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:21.460
feel that I can be myself.

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:22.660
It's a great instrument.

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:26.285
And it really can be part
of so many different sounds.

00:22:08.011 --> 00:22:09.760
The wonderment of the
world is, of course,

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:13.480
based on Middle Eastern and
Islamic and Spanish medieval

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:15.260
themes.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:18.010
So I had a sense that she
would have a connection to it

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:20.010
that nobody else would,
being from Israel.

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:44.080
It really goes straight
into your heart.

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:56.290
I'm working hard for people
to realize that it's not just

00:22:56.290 --> 00:23:00.190
belong to the music of
New Orleans or not just

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:00.780
Benny Goodman.

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:01.860
Clarinetists like
Anat and I have

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:05.160
explored a lot of
ethnic things that

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:09.390
are neither jazz or classical
because in those musics

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:13.590
there's more clarinet, there's
more clarinet in Trinidadian

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:17.340
music, in Colombian
music, in Brazilian music,

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:19.770
in all of the Eastern
European countries.

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:23.040
There's even maybe
more Asian clarinet

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:24.330
than there is jazz clarinet.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:44.920
They're incredible players.

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:47.070
If you look up modern jazz
clarinetists on Google,

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:49.050
Don Byron and Anat Cohen pop up.

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:52.020
The chance to play
along with people

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:56.340
whose music I've listened to
over a decade seems unreal.

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:59.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

